 ## Abstract
 
-Agent Center combined a persistent supervisor with temporary executors under one logical agent to support concurrent execution and unified writeback. We examine responsibility attribution using frozen collaboration records, historical source code, and controlled action-selection experiments. A historical episode attributed executor creation to the center, but changes in dispatch policy and inconsistent tool descriptions prevent attributing its stall to the architecture. We conducted 512 action selections across two models, 16 conditions, eight synthetic scenarios, and two presentation variants, plus 32 incomplete-protocol probes. With consistent descriptions and no legacy rules, both models reached the specified control boundary in 64/64 first actions; the paired difference between unified-agent and component framings was zero. Across all conditions, Sonnet reached 256/256 boundaries and Haiku 253/256. All three non-progressing first actions occurred with legacy history and inserted a legal preparation step. In a separately labeled post hoc continuation, each enqueued an executor on its next action. All incomplete-protocol probes answered that the actor was unspecified. These tests detected no independent disadvantage of unified identity framing and did not reproduce actor misattribution or stalling. They do not identify a training-induced conflict. The findings distinguish identity attribution, action sequencing, and sustained failure, while motivating explicit operational responsibility. The synthetic short tasks do not establish production delivery performance, and the analyses have not been human-verified. (Evidence A00–A19)
+Agent Center grouped a persistent Supervisor and temporary executors into one logical Agent to support concurrent work and unified writeback. We examine why this software-level ownership did not automatically sustain delivery responsibility, using frozen collaboration records, historical source code, an operator account, and controlled action-selection experiments. On 22 July, two agents treated failures of their own execution units as external blockers and revised their responsibility statements after human reminders. A developer diagnosis and prompt revision followed. August records contain both renewed actor misattribution and appropriate ownership; on 4 September, further episodes treated an inadequate executor result as the endpoint of the agent's responsibility and again prompted human correction. The operator recalls frequent early confusion, gradual improvement after repeated reminders, and occasional later errors. The dated records corroborate repeated correction and later occurrences, but do not establish incidence or improvement rates. Separately, 512 synthetic action selections, 32 incomplete-protocol probes, and three post hoc continuations were conducted across two models. With responsibility and recovery obligations already explicit, both models reached the control boundary in 64/64 clean-condition first actions, with zero paired difference between single-sentence identity framings. This demonstrates protocol-following under those conditions, not the absence of historical responsibility breakdowns or a reduced need for human calibration. Thread, workspace, and recovery-interface faults remain excluded from architectural outcome attribution; training-related causes are unidentified. Design evaluation should distinguish execution isolation from continuing goal ownership and measure authorized recovery without reminders and the burden of human intervention. The analyses have not been human-verified. (Evidence A00–A22)
 
 **Keywords:** logical agent; supervisor/executor; accountability; execution lifecycle; agent harness
 
